@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+# Reuse evidence and resolve dependencies at the consuming stage
+
+Apply this policy to both the current-step worker and independent reviewer at
+S01, E01 and B02, including new projects and later research cycles. Read existing
+accepted artifacts and actual results before requesting more preparation. Evidence
+is reusable when its source/configuration, task population, claim scope and target
+remain applicable. Cite the real artifacts; a prior acceptance is not a substitute
+for examining evidence relevant to a changed claim.
+
+## S01: select a sufficient evaluation plan
+
+Start with the project's existing benchmark, metric, split and baseline records.
+When they cover the current prediction and strongest alternative, record the fit
+and proceed to review. Search additional benchmarks only for an identified coverage
+gap. Reuse unchanged source/configuration inspection; do not reopen exhaustive
+literature discovery or require new baseline scores merely to accept a plan.
+Pin the comparison, denominator, protocol, provenance and prospective resource cost.
+Separate known future scoring/qualification work from dependencies required to
+choose a credible plan. An unavailable resource that makes the plan infeasible
+still needs a concrete resolution; do not declare it available or switch to an
+easier test after seeing outcomes.
+
+## E01: reuse a working execution environment
+
+Define the execution target and GPU at intake, using already supplied authority.
+Reuse the confirmed target, interpreter, dependency versions, source revision,
+model/data caches and successful startup receipts for the same configuration.
+Do not recreate environments, reinstall packages, download the same assets or
+repeat identical smoke checks solely because the program entered a new node.
+Recheck affected components when versions/configuration change, access/allocation
+is lost or stale, or an actual failure occurs. Reconcile jobs before dispatch;
+lightweight job/allocation checks do not require repeating environment qualification.
+A routine machine-migration procedure is not part of every run.
+Apply native-scoring-stages.md: a missing scorer blocks work that actually consumes
+scores. Supported independent training/export can retain unscored outputs for
+later scoring. Startup evidence alone does not certify scoring or experiment fit.
+
+## B02: analyze existing results before collecting more
+
+Use traceable historical results, per-example artifacts and accepted audits first.
+Extract supported failures/tradeoffs with their population, denominator and scope.
+Do not rerun a complete historical matrix merely to enter the second research round.
+Aggregate summaries cannot supply absent paired examples, failure frequencies or
+semantic labels. Where such missing evidence is essential, name the exact artifact
+and request the smallest relevant diagnostic on existing natural benchmark inputs,
+with fixed inclusion IDs, outputs, scoring dependencies and explicit limited scope.
+Retain stronger comparisons and confirmation obligations for their proper stage.
+Once archive recovery is exhausted, repeat it only when a new concrete source lead
+appears; otherwise collect the missing evidence through an admitted task.
+
+## Common worker and reviewer criterion
+
+For each current requirement, identify sufficient existing evidence or the exact
+missing artifact, why this stage needs it, and a concrete action to obtain it.
+Do not repeatedly reject unchanged sufficient evidence for future-stage proof.
+Do not submit the same insufficient summary for acceptance without addressing the
+reviewed gap. Evidence sufficiency, independent review, native-task admission and
+coverage contracts remain mandatory. Missing essential evidence is not a PASS;
+keep it explicit and perform the targeted work. Do not invent retry quotas or
+aggregate budgets, bypass the program cursor, manufacture scientific evidence,
+or relabel third-party executors as official. Preserve failed and negative results.
